.TH CTI-CHECKIN-VERDICT 1 "2026-11-18" "cti-agent" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
cti-checkin-verdict \- check the verdict a check-in beat ended with
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cti-checkin-verdict
.RB ( \-\-trace
.I FILE
|
.B \-\-text
.IR FILE )
.RB [ \-\-counts
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-out
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-beat
.IR ID ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Every orchestrator check-in beat ends its final message with one JSON object,
schema
.BR cti-checkin/1 :
an overall status, the state of each of the ten lanes, the alerts it sent, how
many board and memory writes it made, what it deferred, and whether it saw
injected instructions.
.B run\-checkin
runs this command after every beat that exited 0.
.PP
It reads the beat's final message (the trace's last result event, or a
plain-text log), finds the last JSON object naming the schema, and checks it:
strictly against the schema (no unknown fields, only the allowed values, every
lane exactly once, status
.B escalated
exactly when an alert is listed), and, given
.BR \-\-counts ,
against the counts
.BR checkin-bash-guard (1)
kept for the beat, which the model cannot touch: the escalations listed must
equal the alerts it counted, and the board and memory writes must equal its
counts.
.PP
It prints one line,
.B "verdict: valid - ..."
or
.B "verdict: KIND - problem; problem"
where KIND is
.B MISSING
(no verdict),
.B INVALID
(it does not parse or breaks the schema) or
.B MISMATCH
(the guard's counts contradict it).
It sends nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-trace " FILE"
The beat's
.BR fleet-trace (1)
file.
.TP
.BI \-\-text " FILE"
The beat's plain-text output, for a beat run with traces off.
.TP
.BI \-\-counts " FILE"
The guard's counter file for the beat,
.IR $FLEET_HOME/state/checkin-counts/<beat>.json .
Without it only the schema is checked.
.TP
.BI \-\-out " FILE"
Also save the verdict and every problem as JSON, mode 0600.
.B run\-checkin
writes
.IR $FLEET_HOME/state/verdicts/checkin-<beat>.json ,
kept as long as the traces.
.TP
.BI \-\-beat " ID"
The beat id, recorded in
.BR \-\-out .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the full result as JSON instead of one line.
.SH WHAT RUN-CHECKIN DOES WITH IT
A valid verdict is one journal line. A problem is a journal line and a board
line,
.B INFO
to
.BR @all ,
at most once a day for each kind: never mail, and never addressed to the
operator, because the next beat emails any board line addressed to the
operator and a reporting fault is not an incident.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check the newest beat's verdict on the box against the schema (add
.B \-\-counts
with the beat's counter file to check its counts too):
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo cti\-agent cti\-checkin\-verdict \e
  \-\-trace "$(sudo ls \-t /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/traces/checkin\-*.jsonl | head \-1)"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Read what the last saved verdict said, and any problems:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo ls \-t /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/verdicts/ | head \-3
sudo cat /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/verdicts/checkin\-<beat>.json
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The verdict is valid.
.TP
.B 1
It is missing, invalid, or contradicted by the counts.
.TP
.B 2
The input could not be read, or neither or both of
.B \-\-trace
and
.B \-\-text
were given.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR run-checkin (1),
.BR checkin-bash-guard (1),
.BR fleet-trace (1)
